        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac 1 {10}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of [MATH: \\dfrac 1 {10}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, the number <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 0.\\overline{8}]</span> can be expressed as \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + \u2026; = (8)/(10) + (8)/(100) + (8)/(1000) + \u2026]</span>\nWe have <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.]</span> Therefore,\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(8)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 10; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8((1)/(10))^{n}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "For example, the number [MATH: 0.\\overline{8}] can be expressed as\n[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + \u2026; = (8)/(10) + (8)/(100) + (8)/(1000) + \u2026]\nWe have [MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}] and [MATH: r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(8)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 10; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8((1)/(10))^{n}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nA repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of [MATH: \\dfrac 1 {10}.]\n\nContent:\nFor example, the number [MATH: 0.\\overline{8}] can be expressed as\n[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + \u2026; = (8)/(10) + (8)/(100) + (8)/(1000) + \u2026]\nWe have [MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}] and [MATH: r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(8)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 10; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8((1)/(10))^{n}.]"
      },
