          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The given repeating decimal can be written as \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 0.14^(\u2015)|= 0.1444...; = 0.1 + 0.04 + 0.004 + 0.0004 + \u2026; = (1)/(10) + ((4)/(100) + (4)/(1000) + (4)/(10000) + \u2026).]</span></p><p>The series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}]</span> and the common ratio <span class=\"math\">[MATH: r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.]</span> Therefore,\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 0.14^(\u2015)|= (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4 \u22c5 10)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n[MATH: 0.14^(\u2015)|= 0.1444...; = 0.1 + 0.04 + 0.004 + 0.0004 + \u2026; = (1)/(10) + ((4)/(100) + (4)/(1000) + (4)/(10000) + \u2026).]\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term [MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}] and the common ratio [MATH: r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 0.14^(\u2015)|= (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4 \u22c5 10)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n}.]",
