    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nA repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of [MATH: \\dfrac 1 {10}.]\n\nContent:\nFor example, the number [MATH: 0.\\overline{8}] can be expressed as\n[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + \u2026; = (8)/(10) + (8)/(100) + (8)/(1000) + \u2026]\nWe have [MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}] and [MATH: r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 0.8^(\u2015)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(8)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8 \u22c5 (1)/(10) \u22c5 ((1)/(10))^{n} \u22c5 10; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)8((1)/(10))^{n}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Expressing a Repeating Decimal as an Infinite Geometric Series\n\nExample Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: 0.\\overline{23}] as geometric series.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe given repeating decimal can be written as\n[MATH: 0.\\overline{23} = 0.23 + 0.002\\,3 + 0.000\\,023 + \\dots]\n[MATH: 0.\\overline{23} = \\dfrac{23}{100} + \\dfrac{23}{10\\,000} + \\dfrac{23}{1\\,000\\,000}+\\dots]\nwhere the first term is [MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{23}{100}] and the common ratio is [MATH: \\dfrac{1}{100}.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 0.23^(\u2015)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(23)/(100) \u22c5 ((1)/(100))^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)23 \u22c5 (1)/(100) \u22c5 ((1)/(100))^{n \u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)23 \u22c5 (1)/(100) \u22c5 ((1)/(100))^{n} \u22c5 ((1)/(100))^{\u2212 1}; = \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)23((1)/(100))^{n}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 0.64^(\u2015) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)64((1)/(10))^{n}]\nb. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(64)/(100)((1)/(100))^{n}]\nc. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(64)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n}]\nd. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(64)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n}]\ne. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)64((1)/(100))^{n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 0.58^(\u2015) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)5.8((1)/(100))^{n}]\nb. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)58((1)/(1000))^{n}]\nc. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)5.8((1)/(10))^{n}]\nd. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)58((1)/(10))^{n}]\ne. [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)58((1)/(100))^{n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Expressing a Repeating Decimal as the Sum of a Number and a Geometric Series\n\nExample Prompt:\nWrite [MATH: 0.1\\overline{4}] as geometric series.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe given repeating decimal can be written as\n[MATH: 0.14^(\u2015)|= 0.1444...; = 0.1 + 0.04 + 0.004 + 0.0004 + \u2026; = (1)/(10) + ((4)/(100) + (4)/(1000) + (4)/(10000) + \u2026).]\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term [MATH: a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}] and the common ratio [MATH: r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 0.14^(\u2015)|= (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)a_{1}r^{n \u2212 1}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n \u2212 1}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4 \u22c5 10)/(100)((1)/(10))^{n}; = (1)/(10) + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(4)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7.87^(\u2015) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7.8 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(7)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n}]\nb. [MATH: 7.8 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(7)/(100)((1)/(100))^{n}]\nc. [MATH: 7.8 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(8)/(10)((1)/(10))^{n}]\nd. [MATH: 7 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(8)/(9)((1)/(10))^{n}]\ne. [MATH: 7.8 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)(7)/(10)((1)/(100))^{n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 8.81^(\u2015) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 8 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)81((1)/(10))^{n}]\nb. [MATH: 8 + \u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)0.81((1)/(100))^{n}]\nc. [MATH: 8 + \u2211_